/** The MDX component reference appended to every writer system prompt. */
export const COMPONENT_PLAYBOOK = `# Doxa Component Playbook

This is the reference for the MDX components and Markdown features that the Doxa renderer supports. The documentation agent reads it when it writes pages for any project, so it describes only what renders and how to write it. It does not prescribe page types, page structure, navigation, or site layout: choose those from the project being documented.
---

## Component Catalog

Only the components below render. Do not invent component names, and do not write \`import\` statements: the renderer provides every component.

### Cards (\`CardGrid\` + \`Card\`)

Use cards for navigation: related pages, next steps, feature groups, and external resources.

\`\`\`jsx
<CardGrid>
  <Card
    subtitle="Guide"
    title="Authentication"
    description="Create an API key and send it with each request."
    href="/authentication"
  />
  <Card
    title="Configuration"
    href="/configuration"
    icon="settings"
    variant="small"
    description="Every option and its default."
  />
  <Card
    title="Source code"
    href="https://github.com/acme/widget"
    external={true}
  />
</CardGrid>
\`\`\`

**Props**: \`title\` (string), \`subtitle?\` (string), \`description?\` (string), \`href?\` (string), \`icon?\` (an icon name such as \`"rocket"\`, \`"book"\`, \`"code"\`, \`"settings"\`, or any Lucide icon name; camelCase \`bookOpen\` and kebab-case \`book-open\` both work), \`image?\` (string), \`variant?\` (\`"normal"\` | \`"small"\` | \`"image"\`), \`external?\` (boolean), \`className?\` (string)

### Notes (\`Note\`)

Use notes for information readers must not miss: requirements, consequences, deprecations, and security caveats.

\`\`\`jsx
<Note title="Rate limits">
  Each API key can send 100 requests per minute.
</Note>

<Note title="Done" type="success">
  The client is connected.
</Note>

<Note title="Breaking change" type="warning">
  Version 2.0 removes the \`legacy\` option.
</Note>

<Note title="Secrets" type="danger">
  Never commit the API key to source control.
</Note>
\`\`\`

**Props**: \`title?\` (string), \`type?\` (\`"note"\` | \`"success"\` | \`"warning"\` | \`"danger"\`)

There is no \`Warning\`, \`Tip\`, \`Callout\`, \`Banner\`, or \`Alert\` component. Use \`<Note type="warning">\` or \`<Note type="danger">\`.

### Steps (\`Step\` + \`StepItem\`)

Use steps for a sequential procedure. One procedure is one \`<Step>\`. Each step of it is one \`<StepItem title="...">\` inside that \`<Step>\`.

\`\`\`\`jsx
<Step>
  <StepItem title="Install the package">
    Add the client library to the project.

    \`\`\`bash
    npm install @acme/widget
    \`\`\`
  </StepItem>

  <StepItem title="Create a client">
    Create one client and reuse it.

    \`\`\`ts
    import { Widget } from "@acme/widget";

    const widget = new Widget({ apiKey: process.env.WIDGET_API_KEY });
    \`\`\`
  </StepItem>
</Step>
\`\`\`\`

**Step props**: none. \`<Step>\` is only the numbered container.

**StepItem props**: \`title?\` (string), the step heading. Write an action, such as "Configure the provider", not "Step 3".

Rules:

- \`<Step>\` numbers each element directly inside it. Every child of \`<Step>\` must be a \`<StepItem>\`.
- Put the text, code blocks, and \`Tabs\` of a step inside its \`<StepItem>\`, never directly inside \`<Step>\`.
- Never write \`<Step title="...">\`. \`Step\` has no \`title\` prop. The renderer drops the title and numbers every paragraph and code block of the body as a step of its own.
- Never use \`<StepItem>\` outside a \`<Step>\`.
- Leave a blank line before and after code fences inside \`StepItem\`.

Wrong, renders untitled steps with one number per paragraph and code block:

\`\`\`\`jsx
<Step title="Install the package">

\`\`\`bash
npm install @acme/widget
\`\`\`

</Step>

<Step title="Create a client">

Create one client and reuse it.

</Step>
\`\`\`\`

### Tabs (\`Tabs\`, \`TabsList\`, \`TabsTrigger\`, \`TabsContent\`)

Use tabs for alternatives that reach the same goal: package managers, languages, operating systems, or platforms.

\`\`\`\`jsx
<Tabs defaultValue="npm">
  <TabsList>
    <TabsTrigger value="npm">npm</TabsTrigger>
    <TabsTrigger value="pnpm">pnpm</TabsTrigger>
  </TabsList>
  <TabsContent value="npm">

    \`\`\`bash
    npm install @acme/widget
    \`\`\`

  </TabsContent>
  <TabsContent value="pnpm">

    \`\`\`bash
    pnpm add @acme/widget
    \`\`\`

  </TabsContent>
</Tabs>
\`\`\`\`

**Tabs props**: \`defaultValue\` (string matching a trigger \`value\`), \`className?\` (string)
**TabsTrigger, TabsContent props**: \`value\` (string). Each \`TabsContent\` needs a \`TabsTrigger\` with the same \`value\`.

### Mermaid (\`Mermaid\`)

Use Mermaid for flows, sequences, and relationships that prose or a table cannot show clearly.

\`\`\`jsx
<Mermaid
  chart={\`graph TD;
    Client[Client] --> API[API server];
    API --> Queue[Job queue];
    Queue --> Worker[Worker];\`}
/>
\`\`\`

**Props**: \`chart\` (Mermaid syntax string), \`className?\` (string)

### File Tree (\`FileTree\`, \`Folder\`, \`File\`)

Use file trees for directory and file layout.

\`\`\`jsx
<FileTree>
  <Folder name="src" label="Source" defaultOpen>
    <Folder name="routes" label="HTTP routes">
      <File name="users.ts" label="User endpoints" />
    </Folder>
    <File name="index.ts" label="Entry point" />
  </Folder>
</FileTree>
\`\`\`

**Folder props**: \`name\` (string), \`label?\`, \`open?\` (boolean), \`defaultOpen?\` (boolean)  
**File props**: \`name\` (string), \`label?\`

### Video Embed (\`Embed\`)

Use an embed for a YouTube, Vimeo, or Loom video that shows a workflow better than text or images: a walkthrough, a recorded demo, or a talk.

\`\`\`jsx
<Embed url="https://youtu.be/dQw4w9WgXcQ" title="Setup walkthrough" />
\`\`\`

**Props**: \`url\` (string, required: the address from the browser, such as \`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=…\`, \`https://vimeo.com/…\`, or \`https://www.loom.com/share/…\`), \`title?\` (iframe title for screen readers)

The video plays in a 16:9 frame. Any other address renders as a plain link, never as an iframe.

### REST Endpoint Reference (\`ApiEndpoint\`, \`ApiRequestExample\`, \`ApiResponseExample\`, \`ApiParams\`, \`ApiParam\`)

These components render REST request and response metadata. The generator writes them from analyzed API evidence. Never author, rearrange, or repair them by hand.

On a page whose frontmatter carries \`apiMethod\` and \`apiPath\`, the renderer draws the method and path bar, the request and response cards, the parameter groups, and their headings. Write only the introduction and usage notes as prose. Do not add headings for Authorizations, Parameters, Body, or Response.

## Markdown Features

- GitHub-flavored tables
- Checklists
- Blockquotes
- Images with optional titles: \`![Alt text](/assets/diagram.png "Title")\`. Use an \`/assets/…\` path or an \`https\` address, on its own line. Relative paths and HTML \`<img>\` tags do not render.
- Inline and fenced code, with copy buttons and syntax highlighting
- Line numbers with \`showLineNumbers\`
- Line highlighting with \`{3,5-7}\`
- KaTeX math with inline \`$$...$$\` and fenced \`math\` blocks
- Size limits: a code block above 32 KB, or the code after 128 KB of highlighted code on one page, renders as plain text without colors. A line above 2,000 characters renders without colors. A page above 512 KB of MDX renders only a "page too large" notice.

\`\`\`\`md
\`\`\`ts {2} showLineNumbers
const widget = new Widget({
  apiKey: process.env.WIDGET_API_KEY,
  timeoutMs: 5000,
});
\`\`\`
\`\`\`\`

## Anti-patterns

1. Do not invent components or props. Only the components and props in this file render.
2. Do not write \`<Step title="...">\`, one \`<Step>\` per step, or text and code directly inside \`<Step>\`. Write one \`<Step>\` with a \`<StepItem title="...">\` for each step.
3. Do not use steps for lists that are not sequential.
4. Do not overuse notes. More than three or four on one page usually means the prose needs tightening.
5. Do not put cards inside steps or notes inside notes.
6. Do not add a Mermaid diagram where a table or list is clearer.
7. Do not omit the language identifier on a code fence.
8. Do not use \`showLineNumbers\` for one-line commands.
9. Do not author the \`Api*\` endpoint components by hand.
10. Do not put real tokens or secrets in examples or prose.
11. Do not embed a video to show what an image or a short code block shows, or from a provider other than YouTube, Vimeo, or Loom.
12. Do not leave image alt text empty. Describe what the image shows.
13. Do not paste very large generated files or minified code into a code block. Show a short excerpt and link to the full file.

## Composition Rules

- Leave a blank line before and after every code fence inside a component.
- \`StepItem\` belongs directly inside \`Step\`, and \`Step\` holds only \`StepItem\` elements.
- \`Tabs\` can go inside a \`StepItem\` when one step has alternatives.
- \`CardGrid\` belongs at the start or end of a section.
- \`FileTree\` shows physical layout and \`Mermaid\` shows logical flow. Use them together when both help.
- Introduce options in a table before code examples that use them.
- A note explains a consequence or a requirement. It does not repeat the prose around it.
- Put an \`Embed\` or an image on its own line, near the step or section it illustrates.
- \`ApiRequestExample\`, \`ApiResponseExample\`, and \`ApiParams\` belong directly inside their generated \`ApiEndpoint\`.
`;
